outputs.exact_statement:
There is a geometric constant $C_g$ such that $c(\cdot,y)$ and $c(x,\cdot)$ are uniformly semiconcave, with constant $C_g$, including at cut points. Every potential is $D$-Lipschitz and uniformly semiconvex. If $$\mathcal V_u(x)=\{p\in G_x:
                 u(x)+c(x,\exp_xp)+v(\exp_xp)=0\},$$ then $$\label{geo:active-hull}
 \partial u(x)=\operatorname{conv}\mathcal V_u(x).$$ The total active-log graph $\{(x,p):p\in\mathcal V_u(x)\}$ is compact. Every member of the convex hull in <a href="#geo:active-hull" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="geo:active-hull">[geo:active-hull]</a> has a representation with positive weights using at most $n+1$ affinely independent members of $\mathcal V_u(x)$.

Let $p\in G_x$, and let $0<t<s<1$. Along $\gamma(r)=\exp_x(rp)$, let $S_p(r)$ be the Jacobi tensor with $S_p(0)=0$ and $D_rS_p(0)=\mathrm{Id}$, using parallel orthonormal frames. Then $$\label{geo:radial-derivative}
 \frac{d}{dr}H_r(p)=-S_p(r)^{-1}S_p(r)^{-T},
 \qquad
 H_t(p)-H_s(p)\geq\kappa_g(s-t)\mathrm{Id}$$ for a constant $\kappa_g>0$ depending only on the manifold. Furthermore, $$\label{geo:radial-identity}
 A_x(w)w=w$$ on every smooth minimizing branch.

Let $p_i\in G_x$, $m_i>0$, $\sum_i m_i=1$, and $$p=\sum_i m_ip_i,\qquad E=\mathop{\mathrm{span}}\{p_i-p\}.$$ Suppose $t\operatorname{conv}\{p_i\}\subset I(x)$ and $0<t<s<1$. Then $$\label{geo:transverse-slack}
 \left[\frac{A_x(tp)}t-
       \sum_i m_i\frac{A_x(sp_i)}s\right]\bigg|_{E^\perp}
 \geq\kappa_g(s-t)\mathrm{Id}.$$ The same conclusion holds at a limiting time $t$ if the radially contracted hulls lie in $I(x)$ and the displayed Hessians have nonconjugate branch limits.

For every potential $u$, every $x\in M$, and every $p\in\partial u(x)$, one has $p\in G_x$ and $$\label{geo:global-support}
 u(x')\geq u(x)+c(x,\exp_xp)-c(x',\exp_xp)
 \qquad\text{for all }x'\in M.$$ For each $0<t<1$, the projection $F_t:\Gamma_u\to M$ is a homeomorphism; its vectors $tp$ lie in $I(x)$, and its poles are the unique global minimizers of $Q_tu$.

Let $(u,v)$ be a dual pair and $0<t<1$. Then $$\label{geo:intermediate-duality}
 Q_tu=-Q_{1-t}v\in C^{1,1}(M).$$ The $C^{1,1}$ seminorm is bounded solely in terms of $M,g,t$. If $(x_t(z),p_t(z))=F_t^{-1}(z)$ and $\Phi_s$ denotes geodesic flow on $TM$, then $$\label{geo:pole-flow}
 (x_t(z),p_t(z))
   =\Phi_{-t}(z,\mathop{\mathrm{grad}}Q_tu(z)),
 \qquad
 x_t(z)=\exp_z(-t\mathop{\mathrm{grad}}Q_tu(z)).$$ In particular the pole and momentum are Lipschitz, with constants depending only on $M,g,t$.*


*There are also $r_t,b_t>0$, depending only on $M,g,t$, such that every projected pair $z=\exp_x(tp)$ satisfies $$\label{geo:uniform-growth}
 u(x')+c_t(x',z)
 \geq u(x)+c_t(x,z)+b_t d(x,x')^2
 \qquad\text{if }d(x,x')<r_t.$$ These assertions use no density hypothesis. On every compact subinterval of $(0,1)$, their constants can be chosen uniformly.
